docs(spec): withdraw REQ-009's no-canvas prohibition, require the shared canvas

REQ-009 read "the editor MUST NOT implement drag-and-drop or a node-graph
canvas in this version", and the Purpose excluded a canvas outright. That was a
defensible v1 scope constraint, but it now contradicts ADR-065, which makes a
shared canvas the fleet-standard flow-authoring surface. A per-app opt-out
defeats a fleet standard, so the prohibition is withdrawn rather than kept.

Replaced by a uniformity rule instead of a licence to build anything:
- OpenConnector MUST NOT hand-roll a canvas; graph editing MUST consume
  CnGraphCanvas from @conduction/nextcloud-vue (ADR-065).
- A canvas MUST NOT be the sole authoring surface — the typed step list stays as
  the keyboard-operable path (WCAG 2.1 AA 2.1.1; a drag-only editor is not
  keyboard-operable).
- A canvas MUST NOT ship against the current model until edges are explicit:
  `order` is step identity, execution sequence, and the implicit edge set all at
  once, and `branches[].nextStepOrder` / `defaultNextStepOrder` reference it BY
  VALUE — so a dragged edge can silently invalidate every branch target.

The engine exclusion is kept and hardened: ADR-065 relocates execution to
OpenRegister, so no general-purpose engine grows here (ADR-022). Parallel/loops
stay out of scope for the `order`-indexed model and are delivered by the OR
engine's Petri-net core, which supports parallel splits and synchronising joins.

The scenario "reordering uses move controls, not drag-and-drop" asserted that
drag-and-drop was absent, which the rewrite contradicts; it now asserts the real
underlying requirement (reorder achievable by keyboard alone). Adds a scenario
for canvas reuse, marked @e2e exclude until a graph view exists.

openspec validate flow-orchestration --strict: valid.
